<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.opson.maintenance</string>
	</array>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  The app's name is NOT here, and putting it back here does not work.

	  GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE is YES, and a key Xcode synthesizes silently
	  overrides the same key in this file. A CFBundleName set here loses to the
	  generated $(PRODUCT_NAME). This file used to carry one with a comment
	  saying it was load-bearing; it never reached a single build, and a
	  downstream app shipped under its old name because of it.
	  INFOPLIST_KEY_CFBundleName does not rescue it either: Xcode accepts the
	  setting and then ignores it, verified against the built plist.

	  So PRODUCT_NAME in Configuration/Config.xcconfig is the only lever, and it
	  is the name App Store delivery checks for uniqueness (ITMS-90129). Rename
	  the app there. CFBundleDisplayName, the home-screen name, is separate and
	  is INFOPLIST_KEY_CFBundleDisplayName in the target's build settings.
	-->
	<!--
	  There is deliberately no NSUserNotificationsUsageDescription here, and
	  adding one back needs a reason.

	  The template used to ship it with the string "We'd like to send you
	  notifications." in an app that posts none. It cost nothing while unused
	  and was invisible while wrong, because iOS does not display it: the
	  notification prompt is system-written, and the permission comes from
	  UNUserNotificationCenter.requestAuthorization at runtime, not from a
	  purpose string. So the first reader of that sentence is a reviewer
	  comparing declared intent against the App Privacy answers, by which point
	  it is a question to answer rather than a line to edit.

	  If you add notifications, ask for authorization in code and say what you
	  send in the App Store Connect listing. Reach for this key only if you have
	  something specific to declare; a placeholder is worse than its absence.
	-->
	<!--
	  Read-only HealthKit: active energy, resting energy and workouts, for
	  "Expended so far" (HealthKitReader). The prompt only appears when someone
	  taps Connect under More, Watch and health apps, which ships behind the
	  release.health flag. There is deliberately no NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription:
	  Opson never writes to Health, and declaring it would be a claim the
	  binary does not back. The entitlement is com.apple.developer.healthkit in
	  iosApp.entitlements; the bundle id needs the HealthKit capability in the
	  developer portal too, or signing fails.
	-->
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Opson reads your active energy, resting energy and workouts to show what you've expended today. It stays on your phone.</string>
	<!--
	  The export compliance answer, stated once here instead of by hand on every
	  upload. Without it a build finishes processing and then sits in TestFlight
	  as "Missing Compliance", installable by nobody, until someone opens App
	  Store Connect and answers the encryption question. Nothing warns you: the
	  upload succeeds, the build goes VALID, and the testers just never get it.
	  Two downstream apps were sitting in that state at once on 2026-09-25.

	  False is correct for an app whose only encryption is HTTPS through the
	  system's own TLS, which Apple exempts (Category 5 Part 2). That covers
	  crash reporting, telemetry and ad networks. An app that implements or
	  bundles a cipher, or that ships its own TLS, has to change this key and
	  file the paperwork behind it. Check before assuming you inherited the
	  right answer.

	  It does not reach back. The key only affects builds made after it, so any
	  build already sitting in "Missing Compliance" still needs answering by
	  hand in App Store Connect, or a PATCH of usesNonExemptEncryption onto that
	  build through the API.

	  If you ever do have to answer the dialog by hand, the honest-looking
	  option is the wrong one. "Standard encryption algorithms instead of, or in
	  addition to, using or accessing the encryption within Apple's operating
	  system" reads like a description of any app that makes HTTPS calls, but
	  HTTPS through NSURLSession *is* the encryption within Apple's operating
	  system, which that option exists to exclude. Picking it commits you to a
	  CCATS filing and an annual self-classification report. "None of the
	  algorithms mentioned above" is the answer that matches this key.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
	  The barcode scanner (:libraries:scanner:impl, AVFoundation from Kotlin)
	  reads the camera. App Store delivery scans the binary for AVFoundation
	  *references*, not call sites, so this key is required from the build that
	  links the scanner on, even while release.barcode keeps the button hidden;
	  without it the upload is rejected with ITMS-90683. Frames are only read
	  for barcodes on the device and never stored, which is what the string
	  says. Remove the key only together with the scanner.
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Opson uses the camera to read barcodes on food packs. No photos are taken or saved.</string>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>processing</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Custom-scheme deep links. `opson://login-callback` is the browser
	  OAuth return trip and `opson://auth/confirmed` the verify-email
	  redirect — .onOpenURL in iOSApp.swift forwards both to DeepLinkBridge.

	  Universal Links (https://...) require an `Associated Domains`
	  entitlement (`applinks:example.com`) plus an
	  apple-app-site-association file at https://example.com/.well-known/.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.opson</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>opson</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
